<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cao Wei</h1><p class="st-meta">220 CE – 266 CE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The strongest of the Three Kingdoms, founded on Cao Cao's conquest of the north, which ended the Han in 220 and was itself taken over by the Sima family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, holding the last Han emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xian-of-han">Emperor Xian</a>, in his power, reunified northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-guandu">Guandu</a> in 200, and became King of Wei in 216. After his death in early 220 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> forced Emperor Xian to abdicate and proclaimed the Wei dynasty, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wei controlled the populous north. Military agricultural colonies, the tuntian, fed its armies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-rank-system">Nine-Rank system</a> for recommending officials entrenched great families. The court of the Cao family fostered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jian-an-poetry">Jian'an</a> poets. Wei fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a>, absorbed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gongsun-yan">Gongsun Yan</a> in 238 and received envoys from Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himiko">Himiko</a> of the Wa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 249 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a> seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incident-at-the-gaoping-tombs">Gaoping Tombs incident</a>, and the Sima family ruled through puppet emperors. Wei conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a> in 263, and in 266 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-huan">Cao Huan</a> to abdicate, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cao-wei.jpg" alt="Cao Wei" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>220 CE – 266 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cao Pi, Cao Rui, Cao Huan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cao Wei (Three Kingdoms), Wei (Three Kingdoms)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=243&amp;focus=cao-wei" aria-label="Cao Wei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cao Wei lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7769 1191M7855 1179L8008 962M7855 1179L7855 1179M7855 1179L7696 1315M7855 1179L8057 1267M7855 1179L8115 1035M7855 1179L8452 1184"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M8008 962h0M7855 1179h0M7696 1315h0M8057 1267h0M8115 1035h0M8452 1184h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luoyang. <a href="/atlas?year=243&amp;focus=cao-wei">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
